\details{
Let \eqn{K} be the number of tests, \eqn{t = (t_1, \dots, t_K)} a response
profile with \eqn{t_k = 1} for a positive result, \eqn{d = 1} for diseased
and \eqn{d = 0} for healthy, and let \eqn{m} be \code{interaction_order}. For each
disease class the profile probabilities \eqn{\theta_d} are
\deqn{\theta_d = \mathrm{softmax}(X_m \eta_d),}
where \eqn{X_m} is the design matrix whose columns are the functions
\eqn{x_S(t) = \prod_{k \in S} t_k}, one for each set \eqn{S} of at most
\eqn{m} tests, and \eqn{\eta_d} is the vector of the corresponding
class-specific parameters \eqn{\eta_{d,S}}. Equivalently,
\deqn{\theta_{d,t} \propto \exp\Big(\sum_{S} \eta_{d,S}\, x_S(t)\Big),}
where the normalisation runs over all \eqn{2^K} profiles. Log-linear models
of this kind are used for multiple tests with partial verification (Baker, 1995).

The order controls the structure of the dependence:
\itemize{
\item Order 1 (\code{interaction_order = 1}): the tests are conditionally
independent given the disease status.
\item Order 2: each pair of tests may be associated within each class,
but there are no interactions among three or more tests.
\item Order \eqn{K}: no restriction, every profile distribution is
allowed in each class.
}

The parameters have a direct interpretation. With order 1,
\eqn{\eta_{1,k} = \mathrm{logit}(Se_k)} and
\eqn{\eta_{0,k} = \mathrm{logit}(1 - Sp_k)}, so the main effects are the
logits of the sensitivity and of the false positive rate of test \eqn{k}.
A pairwise term \eqn{\eta_{d,\{j,k\}}} is the log odds ratio between tests
\eqn{j} and \eqn{k} within class \eqn{d}, given the results of the other
tests (with order 2 it is the same whatever those results; with higher
orders it is the value when the other tests are negative): zero means that the two tests are
independent within that class, positive values mean that they are positively
associated among the diseased (\eqn{d = 1}) or among the healthy (\eqn{d = 0}).
Ignoring the dependence between tests given the disease status biases the
accuracy (Vacek, 1985; Torrance-Rynard and Walter, 1997; Dendukuri and Joseph, 2001).

The log-likelihood of the data, with \eqn{n_{t,d}} verified subjects of
profile \eqn{t} and disease status \eqn{d}, \eqn{u_t} unverified subjects of
profile \eqn{t}, \eqn{\pi_1 = \pi} the prevalence and \eqn{\pi_0 = 1 - \pi},
is
\deqn{\ell = \sum_t \Big[\sum_d n_{t,d} \log(\pi_d\,\theta_{d,t}\,\lambda_{t,d}) + u_t \log \sum_d \pi_d\,\theta_{d,t}\,(1 - \lambda_{t,d})\Big],}
where \eqn{\lambda_{t,d}} is the probability of verification.
Dependence models with partial verification of this kind are discussed by
Albert and Dodd (2008).
With \code{verification = mar()} the verification probability does not depend on the disease
status given the profile (Rubin, 1976), so the verification term is separated from the
disease model and can be ignored:
\deqn{\ell = \sum_t \Big[\sum_d n_{t,d} \log(\pi_d\,\theta_{d,t}) + u_t \log \sum_d \pi_d\,\theta_{d,t}\Big]}
plus a term that depends only on the verification probabilities.
With \code{verification = mnar()} the verification probability may depend on the disease status
(Baker, 1995; Kosinski and Barnhart, 2003):
\deqn{\mathrm{logit}\,\lambda_{t,d} = \alpha_t + \beta d,}
with one \eqn{\alpha_t} for each verified profile. The parameter \eqn{\beta}
is the log odds ratio of verification between diseased and healthy subjects
with the same profile, and \eqn{\exp(-\beta)} is the odds ratio of disease
between unverified and verified subjects with the same profile. With
\code{verification = mnar(by_profile = TRUE)} there is one \eqn{\beta_t} for each profile.
Profiles that are never verified have \eqn{\lambda_{t,d} = 0}.

With order lower than \eqn{K} the restricted structure also determines the
disease proportion in the never-verified profiles, and makes \eqn{\beta}
formally estimable. This information comes from the assumed structure, not
from the data, and it is fragile if the structure is wrong (Albert and Dodd, 2004). With order
\eqn{K} the model imposes no restriction and \eqn{\beta} is not identified (Gustafson, 2015).
}
\section{Priors on sensitivity and specificity}{

The model is fitted on the main effects \eqn{\eta_{d,k}}, and
\code{sensitivity_prior} and \code{specificity_prior} are translated into priors on
them. With \code{interaction_order = 1} the main effects are the logits of the
accuracy,
\deqn{\eta_{1,k} = \mathrm{logit}\,\mathrm{Se}_k, \qquad
      \eta_{0,k} = \mathrm{logit}(1 - \mathrm{Sp}_k),}
so a prior \eqn{\mathrm{Beta}(a, b)} given to \code{sensitivity_prior} is the
prior of \eqn{\mathrm{Se}_k}, and one given to \code{specificity_prior} is the
prior of \eqn{\mathrm{Sp}_k} (that is, \eqn{\mathrm{Beta}(b, a)} on the
false positive rate \eqn{1 - \mathrm{Sp}_k}).

With \code{interaction_order} of 2 or more the main effects are logits of
conditional probabilities: the probability that test \eqn{k} is positive
among the subjects of class \eqn{d} who are negative on all the other
tests,
\deqn{\mathrm{expit}\,\eta_{d,k} = P(T_k = 1 \mid D = d,\ T_j = 0
      \ (j \neq k)).}
So \code{sensitivity_prior} acts on the conditional sensitivity
\deqn{P(T_k = 1 \mid D = 1,\ T_j = 0 \ (j \neq k)) = \mathrm{expit}\,\eta_{1,k},}
and \code{specificity_prior} on the conditional specificity
\deqn{P(T_k = 0 \mid D = 0,\ T_j = 0 \ (j \neq k)) = 1 - \mathrm{expit}\,\eta_{0,k},}
not on the marginal \eqn{\mathrm{Se}_k} and \eqn{\mathrm{Sp}_k}. The
priors implied on the marginal sensitivity and specificity also depend on
\code{interactions_prior}. With the default priors and three tests they are
close to the uniform, but the interactions move the marginal sensitivity
slightly up and the marginal specificity slightly down (medians about 0.54
and 0.46). An informative \code{sensitivity_prior} or \code{specificity_prior} should
be set with this in mind.
}

